{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Forasmuch as I have seen the face of Elohim.</b> Just as one who sees the face of Elohim three times a year — as it is written (Exodus 23:15), ״וְלֹא יֵרָאוּ פָנַי רֵיקָם״ — for he must bring burnt-offerings of appearance, so too I have no desire to see your face empty-handed; therefore I ask that you accept my gift. For I acknowledge that the blessings are not mine, for you are a lord over your brothers, and every lord and prince is called Elohim, as it is written (Exodus 7:1), ״רְאֵה נְתַתִּיךָ אֱלֹהִים לְפַרְעֹה״. Therefore he likened seeing his face to seeing the face of Elohim. And therefore ״קַח נָא אֶת בִּרְכָתִי אֲשֶׁר הֻבָאת לָךְ״ (Genesis 33:11) — and he did not say this about the gift, but rather about the blessings that my father blessed me. ״קַח נָא אוֹתָהּ לְךָ״, for it is yours, and it was brought to you, for my father, when he blessed me, had his mind set upon you. And if you would say: if the blessings are not mine, then from where do I have all this property? — concerning this he said ״כִּי חַנַּנִי אֱלֹהִים״ (ibid.), that He gave it to me by way of grace — like one who shows grace to the poor — not by way of obligation on account of the blessings. And behold, the Glory of Israel does not deceive: even though outwardly he said ״רָאִיתִי פָנֶיךָ כִּרְאֹת פְּנֵי אֱלֹהִים״ (Genesis 33:10), none the less, in his heart the intention was: just as I was terrified at night when I saw the face of Elohim — your angel — so was I terrified at seeing your face."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:53.917393617Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}